    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"The alternating zones of punctations (impressed points or dots) and sharp black inserted stones on this object create a texture reminiscent of a crocodilian’s rough skin, a similarity the maker enhanced by adding the crocodilian head in profile at the top of the grater. Some scholars suspect that the gritty, abrasive surface was used to descale fish. The object also may have\n\nfunctioned as a grater for root vegetables such as bitter manioc, a dietary staple in the region. Bitter manioc naturally contains poisonous cyanogenic glucosides, requiring careful processing of the tuber before ingestion. The treatment includes peeling, grating, and then squeezing out the toxic juices. Manioc graters are much larger today, as it would be time-consuming to prepare more than a small quantity with a grater of this size. Perhaps this grater was made for preparing other plants on a more\n\nindividual scale.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA2_20-T6-2_CLA_FA_9_17_25.pdf - Day 1 installation"
        },
        {
